\section{Experimental Setup and Metrics}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Benchmark, Models, and Evaluation}\label{sec:benchmark}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a collision-aware extension of LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero} with per-timestep collision detection. We use the \textit{object\_pudding} suite (Level~I): four tabletop pick-and-place tasks where non-target objects create collision hazards. We evaluate two VLA families: \textbf{Pi0} (${\sim}$2.5B parameters)~\cite{black2024pi0}, which generates continuous actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}, and \textbf{Pi0.5}, which extends Pi0 with broader pre-training and higher task capability. Table~\ref{tab:configurations} summarizes all seven configurations. Each is evaluated with 50 episodes per task (200 total); Pi0.5 multi-seed experiments use 3 seeds (600 episodes). We report Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR), the fraction of collision-free episodes, and Task Success Rate (TSR), the fraction of episodes achieving task completion~\cite{hu2025vlsa,zhang2025safevla}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Training configurations. Trainable parameter counts exclude the frozen backbone. The SDF auxiliary heads add $<$660K parameters to the ${\sim}$33.5M LoRA budget.}
\label{tab:configurations}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Configuration & Description & Trainable Params \\
\midrule
Pi0 Full-FT & Full fine-tune, flow-matching loss & ${\sim}$2.5B \\
Pi0 LoRA & LoRA adapters only & ${\sim}$33.5M \\
Pi0+A1 (SDF aux)\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & LoRA + SDF decoder + EE extractor & ${\sim}$658K \\
Pi0+A3 (learned SDF) & LoRA + SDF decoder w/ gradient refinement & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
Pi0.5 (pre-trained) & No fine-tuning & 0 \\
Pi0.5 LoRA & LoRA fine-tune on Pi0.5 & ${\sim}$33.5M \\
Pi0.5+A1 (SDF aux) & LoRA + SDF aux on Pi0.5, $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$ & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{2pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}LoRA adapter weights were not loaded due to a parameter-name mismatch; only the auxiliary SDF and EE heads were trained.
\end{table}

\subsection{Displacement-Filtered CAR}\label{sec:dfcar}

Standard CAR conflates genuine avoidance with action collapse: a robot that never moves registers as ``safe.'' We define an episode as \emph{action-collapsed} when its maximum displacement $d_{\max}(e) < \tau_d$, where $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. The displacement-filtered CAR excludes these episodes:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car_f}
\text{CAR}_\text{f} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e) \;\land\; d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}{|\{e : d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}
\end{equation}
where $\text{safe}(e)$ indicates zero collisions. The displacement distribution is sharply bimodal---collapsed episodes below 1\,$\mu$m, active episodes above 200\,mm---so any $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm yields identical results (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_threshold}). CAR$_\text{f}$ detects fake safety (Pi0+A1: 91.5\% $\to$ ${\approx}$\,0\%) and preserves genuine safety (Pi0.5: CAR$_\text{f}$ = raw CAR = 28.5\%).\footnote{For Pi0{+}A1, displacement is measured at the obstacle rather than the end-effector, as the original evaluation infrastructure recorded obstacle displacement only. The conclusion is independently corroborated by TSR{=}0\%.} Because CAR$_\text{f}$ operates at the episode level, it detects partial collapse invisible to model-level TSR filtering.

\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Training}\label{sec:sdf_aux}

We augment LoRA-adapted VLAs with two lightweight auxiliary heads: an SDF decoder (${\sim}$395K params) predicting signed distance to the nearest obstacle surface, and an EE position extractor (${\sim}$263K params). The total loss is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:total_loss}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda \left( \mathcal{L}_\text{SDF} + \mathcal{L}_\text{EE} \right)
\end{equation}
where $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$. Training uses AdamW with cosine decay ($2.5 \times 10^{-5} \to 2.5 \times 10^{-6}$) for 30K steps with batch size 32. For Pi0.5+A1, Phase~1 (steps 0--5K) trains only auxiliary heads; Phase~2 (5K--30K) unfreezes LoRA for joint optimization. Architecture details are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_training}. In the Pi0+A1 configuration, a parameter-name mismatch prevented LoRA loading; only auxiliary heads (${\sim}$658K params) were trained, causing complete action collapse (TSR\,=\,0\%).

\subsection{Oracle SDF Refinement}\label{sec:oracle_setup}

To test the upper bound of EE-only refinement (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}), we replace the learned SDF with ground-truth distance fields from the simulation geometry. Three oracle variants---adaptive step size, fixed step size, and feature-space gradient---are each evaluated for 160 episodes (4 tasks $\times$ 40). Variant details are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_oracle}.

\section{The Fake Safety Problem}\label{sec:fake_safety}

Standard CAR assumes that collision-free episodes reflect deliberate avoidance. This assumption fails under action collapse: a robot that never moves will never collide, yet it has not avoided anything. We show that action collapse dominates the safety scores of Pi0-family models.

\subsection{Action Collapse Contamination}\label{sec:collapse}

