\section{Experimental Setup and Metrics}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Benchmark and Models}\label{sec:benchmark}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, the \textit{object\_pudding} suite (Level~I): four tabletop pick-and-place tasks with collision hazards, using per-timestep collision detection. Two VLA families are tested: \textbf{Pi0} (${\sim}$2.5B)~\cite{black2024pi0} with flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}, and \textbf{Pi0.5} with broader pre-training. Table~\ref{tab:configurations} lists all configurations. Each uses 50 episodes per task (200 total); Pi0.5 multi-seed runs use 3 seeds (600 episodes). We report Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR), the fraction of collision-free episodes, and Task Success Rate (TSR), the fraction achieving task completion~\cite{hu2025vlsa,zhang2025safevla}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Training configurations. Trainable parameters exclude the frozen backbone.}
\label{tab:configurations}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Configuration & Description & Trainable Params \\
\midrule
Pi0 Full-FT & Full fine-tune, flow-matching loss & ${\sim}$2.5B \\
Pi0 LoRA & LoRA adapters only & ${\sim}$33.5M \\
Pi0+A1 (SDF aux)\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & LoRA + SDF decoder + EE extractor & ${\sim}$658K \\
Pi0+A3 (learned SDF) & LoRA + SDF decoder w/ gradient refinement & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
Pi0.5 (pre-trained) & No fine-tuning & 0 \\
Pi0.5 LoRA & LoRA fine-tune on Pi0.5 & ${\sim}$33.5M \\
Pi0.5+A1 (SDF aux) & LoRA + SDF aux on Pi0.5, $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$ & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{2pt}
\raggedright\footnotesize\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}LoRA weights not loaded due to parameter-name mismatch; only auxiliary heads trained.
\end{table}

\subsection{Displacement-Filtered CAR}\label{sec:dfcar}

Standard CAR conflates genuine avoidance with action collapse. We define an episode as \emph{action-collapsed} when $d_{\max}(e) < \tau_d$ ($\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm) and exclude such episodes:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car_f}
\text{CAR}_\text{f} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e) \;\land\; d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}{|\{e : d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}
\end{equation}
The displacement distribution is sharply bimodal---collapsed episodes below 1\,$\mu$m, active episodes above 200\,mm---so any $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm yields identical results (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_threshold}). CAR$_\text{f}$ detects fake safety (Pi0+A1: 91.5\% $\to$ ${\approx}$\,0\%) and preserves genuine safety (Pi0.5: CAR$_\text{f}$ = CAR = 28.5\%).\footnote{For Pi0{+}A1, displacement is measured at the obstacle rather than the end-effector. The conclusion is independently corroborated by TSR{=}0\%.} Because CAR$_\text{f}$ operates at the episode level, it detects partial collapse invisible to model-level TSR filtering.

\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Training}\label{sec:sdf_aux}

We augment LoRA-adapted VLAs with two lightweight auxiliary heads: an SDF decoder (${\sim}$395K params) and an EE position extractor (${\sim}$263K params). The total loss is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:total_loss}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda \left( \mathcal{L}_\text{SDF} + \mathcal{L}_\text{EE} \right)
\end{equation}
where $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$. Phase~1 (steps 0--5K) trains only auxiliary heads; Phase~2 (5K--30K) unfreezes LoRA for joint optimization with AdamW and cosine decay. Architecture and hyperparameter details are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_training}. In the Pi0+A1 configuration, a parameter-name mismatch prevented LoRA loading; only auxiliary heads (${\sim}$658K params) were trained, causing action collapse (TSR\,=\,0\%).

\subsection{Oracle SDF Refinement}\label{sec:oracle_setup}

To test the upper bound of EE-only refinement (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}), we replace the learned SDF with ground-truth distance fields. Three oracle variants---adaptive step size, fixed step size, and feature-space gradient---are each evaluated for 160 episodes. Details are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_oracle}.

\section{The Fake Safety Problem}\label{sec:fake_safety}

Standard CAR assumes that collision-free episodes reflect deliberate avoidance. This assumption fails under action collapse: a robot that never moves will never collide, yet it has not avoided anything.

\subsection{Action Collapse Contamination}\label{sec:collapse}

Table~\ref{tab:fake_safety} and Figure~\ref{fig:displacement} summarize the findings. All three Pi0 configurations exhibit pervasive fake safety: over 99\% of safe episodes have displacement below 0.1\,mm, and TSR is at or near 0\%.\footnote{Pi0-family displacement values report obstacle displacement from the original evaluation protocol; Pi0.5 values report end-effector displacement from the rotation-corrected protocol (\S\ref{sec:dfcar}). In both cases, sub-millimeter displacement indicates action collapse, independently confirmed by TSR.} Applying CAR$_\text{f}$ collapses all three to ${\approx}$\,0\%. Pi0.5 validates the metric from the opposite direction: all 57 safe episodes exceed 288\,mm displacement, yielding 0\% fake safe rate and CAR$_\text{f}$ equal to raw CAR (28.5\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Fake safety decomposition on SafeLIBERO. CAR$_\text{f}$ excludes episodes with displacement $< \tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. ``Fake Safe'' is the fraction of safe episodes below $\tau_d$.}
\label{tab:fake_safety}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccccl}
\toprule
Method & Ep. & CAR (\%) & TSR (\%) & Fake Safe (\%) & Real Safe & CAR$_\text{f}$ (\%) & Disp.\,(m) \\
\midrule
Pi0 Full-FT  & 200 & 83.0 & 0.5 & 99.4 & 1/166 & $\approx$\,0 & 0.027 \\
Pi0 LoRA     & 200 & 82.0 & 0.0 & ${\sim}$99 & ${\sim}$1/164 & $\approx$\,0 & 0.010 \\
Pi0+A1 (SDF) & 800 & 91.5 & 0.0 & 99.6 & 3/732 & ---$^*$ & 0.003 \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 (3-seed)  & 600 & 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6 & 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5 & --- & --- & --- & 0.367 \\
Pi0.5 (reeval)  & 200 & 28.5 & 71.5 & 0.0 & 57/57 & 28.5 & 0.367 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\footnotesize $^*$\,Denominator $<5$; statistically unreliable. Values prefixed with $\sim$ are estimated from mean displacement.}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
